ts":[{"text":"let","kind":"keyword"},{"text":" ","kind":"text"},{"text":"listen","kind":"identifier"},{"text":": ","kind":"text"},{"preciseIdentifier":"s:7IvorMXL9MXLListenV","text":"MXLListen","kind":"typeIdentifier"},{"text":"?","kind":"text"}],"kind":"symbol"},"doc://IvorMXL/documentation/IvorMXL/MXLListen":{"identifier":"doc:\/\/IvorMXL\/documentation\/IvorMXL\/MXLListen","role":"symbol","navigatorTitle":[{"text":"MXLListen","kind":"identifier"}],"type":"topic","kind":"symbol","fragments":[{"text":"struct","kind":"keyword"},{"text":" ","kind":"text"},{"text":"MXLListen","kind":"identifier"}],"abstract":[{"type":"text","text":"The listen and listening types, new in Version 4.0, specify different ways that a score following or machine"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"listening application can interact with a performer. The listen type handles interactions that are specific to"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"a note. If multiple child elements of the same type are present, they should have distinct player and\/or time-"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"only attributes."}],"url":"\/documentation\/ivormxl\/mxllisten","title":"MXLListen"},"doc://IvorMXL/documentation/IvorMXL/MXLNote":{"kind":"symbol","role":"symbol","url":"\/documentation\/ivormxl\/mxlnote","title":"MXLNote","fragments":[{"text":"struct","kind":"keyword"},{"text":" ","kind":"text"},{"text":"MXLNote","kind":"identifier"}],"navigatorTitle":[{"text":"MXLNote","kind":"identifier"}],"abstract":[{"type":"text","text":"Notes are the most common type of MusicXML data. The MusicXML format distinguishes between elements used for"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"sound information and elements used for notation information (e.g., tie is used for sound, tied for notation)."},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"Thus grace notes do not have a duration element. Cue notes have a duration element, as do forward elements,"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"text":"but no tie elements. Having these two types of information available can make interchange easier, as some","type":"text"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"programs handle one type of information more readily than the other."}],"type":"topic","identifier":"doc:\/\/IvorMXL\/documentation\/IvorMXL\/MXLNote"},"doc://IvorMXL/documentation/IvorMXL":{"url":"\/documentation\/ivormxl","kind":"symbol","type":"topic","role":"collection","title":"IvorMXL","identifier":"doc:\/\/IvorMXL\/documentation\/IvorMXL","abstract":[{"type":"text","text":"A MusicXML parser, normalizer, validator, and formatter."}]}}}